export const D1={title:'The dental team, specialties and history',hook:'In 1885 Dr. C. Edmund Kells of New Orleans hired one of the first dental assistants so he could work with four hands instead of two. Today’s dental team—dentists, hygienists, assistants, laboratory technicians and business staff—still works that way. Knowing who does what, which specialties exist and how the profession developed is 10% of the Dental Science test.',objectives:['Describe the structure of the Dental Science event, its test plan and its skills.','Compare the education, credentials and duties of each dental team member.','Identify the twelve ADA-recognized dental specialties.','Recognize major people and events in the history of dentistry.'],sections:[
['The Dental Science event',[
'Dental Science has two rounds. **Round One** is a **50-question multiple-choice test in 60 minutes**, with no time announcements at ILC—you track your own time. The test plan covers the dental team, specialties and history (10%), dental anatomy and physiology (10%), dental pathology (10%), tooth morphology (10%), infection control and occupational safety (10%), **chairside assisting and instruments, including restorative and laboratory materials (30%)**, radiology (10%) and medical emergencies (10%). Questions come from the official references: Boyd’s *Dental Instruments: A Pocket Guide* and Robinson’s *Modern Dental Assisting*, with *DHO: Health Science* used for skills.',
'Top scorers advance to **Round Two**, a skills assessment presented as a **written scenario**: Skill I instrument identification (15 minutes), II aspirating anesthetic syringe (7), III seat, prepare and dismiss a patient (10), IV surface barriers (7), V oral hygiene instruction (7), VI alginate impressions (7), VII ultrasonic cleaner (5) and VIII PPE donning and doffing (5). Instrument identification may be given with the Round One test. Body fluids are simulated, steps must be performed or verbalized if equipment is unavailable, and judges stop any step that risks harm. To be recognized as an award winner, competitors need **70% or higher on the combined Round Two skills** (excluding the test and instrument identification). Final rank adds the test and skill scores; ties go to the higher test score. Competitors bring photo ID, two #2 pencils, non-latex and utility gloves, a disposable mask and gown and protective eyewear.']],
['The dental team',[
'The **dentist** holds a **DDS** (Doctor of Dental Surgery) or **DMD** (Doctor of Dental Medicine)—equivalent degrees—and is licensed by the state to diagnose, plan treatment and perform procedures. The **registered dental hygienist (RDH)** completes at least an associate degree, passes national and clinical board exams and is state-licensed to perform prophylaxis (cleanings), scaling and root planing, patient education, radiographs and, in many states, local anesthesia. The **dental assistant** works chairside, prepares rooms and instruments, takes radiographs, manages infection control and educates patients; the **Certified Dental Assistant (CDA)** credential is earned through the **Dental Assisting National Board (DANB)**, and **expanded function dental assistants (EFDAs)** perform additional duties as state law allows. The **dental laboratory technician** fabricates crowns, bridges, dentures and appliances from the dentist’s prescription. **Business staff** schedule, manage records, insurance and billing. Each state’s **dental practice act**, enforced by the state board of dentistry, defines which duties each member may perform and under what **supervision** (direct supervision means the dentist is in the office and checks the work).',
sort('Typical duty of which team member?',['Dental hygienist','Dental assistant','Dental laboratory technician'],[['Scaling and root planing',0],['Passing instruments during a restoration',1],['Fabricating a crown from a prescription',2],['Preparing a treatment room and setting up trays',1],['Periodontal charting and prophylaxis',0],['Making a complete denture',2]])]],
['The twelve recognized specialties',[
'The American Dental Association’s National Commission recognizes twelve specialties, each requiring advanced education after dental school:',
{t:'table',caption:'ADA-recognized dental specialties',head:['Specialty','Focus'],rows:[['Dental anesthesiology','Anesthesia, sedation and pain management'],['Dental public health','Community oral health, prevention and policy'],['Endodontics','Dental pulp and root canal treatment'],['Oral and maxillofacial pathology','Diagnosis of oral diseases, often by biopsy'],['Oral and maxillofacial radiology','Interpretation of images such as CBCT'],['Oral and maxillofacial surgery','Extractions, implants, jaw and facial surgery'],['Oral medicine','Oral care for medically complex patients'],['Orofacial pain','Pain of the jaw, face and TMJ'],['Orthodontics and dentofacial orthopedics','Alignment of teeth and jaws'],['Pediatric dentistry','Care of children and patients with special needs'],['Periodontics','Gums, bone and supporting structures; implants'],['Prosthodontics','Replacement of teeth with crowns, bridges, dentures']]},
match('Match the case to the specialist',[['Root canal therapy on a molar','Endodontist'],['Braces for crowding','Orthodontist'],['Deep periodontal pockets and bone loss','Periodontist'],['Complete dentures for an edentulous patient','Prosthodontist'],['Removal of impacted third molars','Oral and maxillofacial surgeon'],['A 4-year-old with early childhood caries','Pediatric dentist']])]],
['History of dentistry',[
'Dentistry has ancient roots. **Hesy-Re**, an Egyptian scribe about 5,000 years ago, is the earliest named person described as a dental practitioner. Greek writers including **Hippocrates** and **Aristotle** wrote about teeth and extraction. In 1728, French surgeon **Pierre Fauchard** published *The Surgeon Dentist*, earning the title **father of modern dentistry**. In the United States, **Paul Revere** made dentures and identified a fallen soldier by his dental work—an early example of forensic dentistry. The world’s first dental college, the **Baltimore College of Dental Surgery**, opened in **1840** and awarded the DDS degree. **Horace Wells** used **nitrous oxide** for dental pain control in 1844. **G. V. Black** standardized cavity preparation and the classification of caries still used today and studied mottled enamel later linked to fluoride. **Lucy Hobbs Taylor** became the first woman to graduate from a dental college (1866), and **Ida Gray Nelson Rollins** the first African American woman to earn a DDS (1890). **Wilhelm Roentgen** discovered X-rays in 1895, and **C. Edmund Kells** took the first dental radiograph in the United States and employed early dental assistants. **Alfred Fones** trained the first dental hygienist, Irene Newman, and opened the first hygiene school in 1913. **Juliette Southard** founded the American Dental Assistants Association.',
flip('Who am I?',[['Pierre Fauchard','Father of modern dentistry; wrote The Surgeon Dentist (1728)'],['Horace Wells','Used nitrous oxide for dental pain control (1844)'],['G. V. Black','Classified caries and standardized cavity preparation'],['C. Edmund Kells','First U.S. dental radiograph; early use of dental assistants'],['Alfred Fones','Father of dental hygiene; trained Irene Newman'],['Lucy Hobbs Taylor','First woman to graduate from a dental college (1866)'],['Juliette Southard','Founder of the American Dental Assistants Association'],['Baltimore College of Dental Surgery','First dental college (1840)']])]],
['Ethics, law and professionalism',[
'Dental professionals follow the **ADA Principles of Ethics**: **patient autonomy** (self-determination and informed consent), **nonmaleficence** (do no harm), **beneficence** (do good), **justice** (fairness) and **veracity** (truthfulness). Patients give **informed consent** after learning the diagnosis, treatment options, risks and costs. **HIPAA** protects patient information, so records are kept secure and conversations private. **Dental records** are legal documents: entries are accurate, dated and signed, and errors are corrected with a single line, the date and initials—never erased. Abuse and neglect, including signs seen in the head and neck, must be reported as required by state law. Professional appearance, punctuality and teamwork matter in every role.',
kase('Within the scope?',{a:{text:'A busy dentist asks a newly hired dental assistant, who has no expanded-function training, to place a permanent composite restoration. What should the assistant do?',choices:[['Explain that the duty is outside their legal scope and offer to assist instead','b','Correct. The state dental practice act defines allowed duties.',true],['Place the restoration to save time','b','Performing duties outside the practice act is illegal and unsafe.',false]]},b:{text:'Later, the assistant charts the wrong tooth number. How is the record corrected?',choices:[['Draw a single line through the error, write the correction, date and initial it','c','Correct. Records are legal documents.',true],['Use correction fluid','c','Erasing or obscuring entries is never acceptable.',false]]},c:{text:'The assistant learns which duties require additional credentials in their state and plans to pursue the CDA.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Round One: 50 questions in 60 minutes; Round Two scenario skills need 70% combined to be recognized; chairside and instruments are 30% of the test.','Dentist (DDS/DMD), licensed RDH, dental assistant (CDA via DANB), lab technician and business staff; scope set by the state practice act.','Twelve ADA specialties; Fauchard is the father of modern dentistry; Baltimore 1840; Wells nitrous oxide 1844; Black’s classifications.'],terms:[['Dental practice act','State law that defines dental duties and supervision.'],['DDS/DMD','Equivalent dentist degrees.'],['Expanded function dental assistant','Assistant credentialed for added duties allowed by state law.']],mcqs:[q('How many questions are on the Round One test?','50 in 60 minutes','100 in 90 minutes','25 in 30 minutes','75 in 60 minutes','There are no time announcements at ILC.'),q('Which test area has the largest weight?','Chairside assisting/instruments including materials (30%)','Radiology (10%)','Dental history (10%)','Medical emergencies (10%)','Chairside and instruments make up 30%.'),q('Who is called the father of modern dentistry?','Pierre Fauchard','G. V. Black','Horace Wells','Paul Revere','He wrote The Surgeon Dentist in 1728.'),q('The CDA credential is awarded by:','The Dental Assisting National Board','The American Medical Association','The state hospital association','HOSA','DANB certifies dental assistants.'),q('Which specialist performs root canal treatment?','Endodontist','Periodontist','Orthodontist','Prosthodontist','Endodontics treats the pulp.'),q('The first dental college opened in 1840 in:','Baltimore','Boston','Paris','London','The Baltimore College of Dental Surgery awarded the DDS.'),q('Which score is required to be recognized as an award winner?','70% or higher on the combined Round Two skills','50% on the test','90% on instrument ID','100% on every skill','Instrument ID and the test are excluded.'),q('How is an error in a dental record corrected?','Single line through it, correction, date and initials','Correction fluid','Erasing it','Tearing out the page','Records are legal documents.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the members of the dental team, their education and credentials, and how state law shapes their duties. Then name four dental specialties and the care each provides.',rubric:[['Team members and duties',['dentist','hygienist','assistant','laboratory','business']],['Education and credentials',['dds','dmd','rdh','cda','danb','licens']],['Legal scope',['practice act','state board','supervision','scope']],['Four specialties',['endodont','orthodont','periodont','prosthodont','oral and maxillofacial','pediatric']]],model:'The dentist earns a DDS or DMD and is state-licensed to diagnose and treat oral disease; the registered dental hygienist completes at least an associate degree and licensing exams to perform cleanings, scaling and root planing, radiographs and patient education; the dental assistant prepares rooms, assists chairside, processes instruments, takes radiographs and may earn the CDA through DANB; the dental laboratory technician fabricates crowns, bridges and dentures from prescriptions; and business staff manage scheduling, records and billing. Each state’s dental practice act, enforced by the board of dentistry, defines what each member may do and the level of supervision required, so an assistant may perform expanded functions only with proper credentials. Endodontists perform root canal treatment, orthodontists align teeth and jaws, periodontists treat gum disease and place implants, and prosthodontists replace missing teeth with crowns, bridges and dentures.'},sources:[RULES,SPEC,DANB,ADEA]};

export const D2={title:'Head, neck and oral anatomy',hook:'A single injection near the mandibular foramen can numb half the lower jaw, lip and tongue. That works because of anatomy: the inferior alveolar nerve enters the mandible there. Dental professionals rely on the bones, muscles, glands and nerves of the head to examine, anesthetize and treat safely.',objectives:['Identify the bones of the skull, the parts of the mandible and the temporomandibular joint.','Describe the muscles of mastication and their actions.','Locate the salivary glands, their ducts and the landmarks of the oral cavity.','Trace the trigeminal nerve branches that supply the teeth.'],sections:[
['Bones of the skull',[
'The skull has **8 cranial bones** (frontal, two parietal, occipital, two temporal, sphenoid and ethmoid) and **14 facial bones** (two nasal, two lacrimal, two zygomatic, two maxillae, two palatine, two inferior nasal conchae, the vomer and the mandible). The paired **maxillae** form the upper jaw, hold the maxillary teeth in the **alveolar process**, form most of the hard palate and contain the **maxillary sinuses**—which is why sinus pain can be felt in upper molars. The **mandible** is the only movable bone of the skull. Its parts include the **body**, the **ramus**, the **angle**, the **coronoid process** (where the temporalis attaches), the **condyle** (which forms the jaw joint), the **mental foramen** (exit for the mental nerve near the premolars), the **mandibular foramen** (entry for the inferior alveolar nerve on the inner ramus) and the **symphysis**, the midline where the two halves fused in infancy.',
label('skull','Explore the skull and mandible',[['temporal','Temporal bone','Contains the glenoid (mandibular) fossa of the TMJ.'],['zygomatic','Zygomatic bone','Cheekbone; the masseter arises from the zygomatic arch.'],['maxilla','Maxilla','Upper jaw holding maxillary teeth; contains the maxillary sinus.'],['body','Body of the mandible','Horizontal portion holding mandibular teeth.'],['angle','Angle of the mandible','Where the body meets the ramus; masseter inserts here.'],['ramus','Ramus','Vertical portion; the mandibular foramen is on its inner surface.'],['coronoid','Coronoid process','Anterior projection where the temporalis attaches.'],['condyle','Condyle','Rounded process forming the TMJ.'],['tmj','Temporomandibular joint','Hinge and gliding joint between condyle and temporal bone.'],['mental','Mental foramen','Opening for the mental nerve and vessels near the premolars.']])]],
['The temporomandibular joint and chewing muscles',[
'The **temporomandibular joint (TMJ)** connects the mandibular condyle to the **glenoid (mandibular) fossa** of the temporal bone, with an **articular disc** between them. It moves in two ways: a **hinge** (rotational) motion when the mouth first opens and a **gliding** (translational) motion as the condyle moves forward onto the **articular eminence**. **Temporomandibular disorders (TMD)** cause pain, clicking and limited opening, often linked to clenching or bruxism. Four paired **muscles of mastication**, supplied by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve, move the jaw: the **temporalis** elevates and retracts; the **masseter**, the strongest, elevates; the **medial pterygoid** elevates and helps side-to-side movement; and the **lateral pterygoid** depresses (opens), protrudes and moves the jaw side to side.',
label('mastication','Muscles of mastication',[['temporalis','Temporalis','Fan-shaped; elevates and retracts the mandible.'],['masseter','Masseter','Strongest chewing muscle; elevates the mandible.'],['medial','Medial pterygoid','Elevates and assists lateral movement.'],['lateral','Lateral pterygoid','Opens, protrudes and moves the jaw side to side.']]),
match('Match the muscle to its main action',[['Masseter','Elevates the mandible (closes)'],['Temporalis','Elevates and retracts'],['Lateral pterygoid','Depresses and protrudes'],['Medial pterygoid','Elevates and assists side-to-side'],['Buccinator (facial muscle)','Holds food against the teeth'],['Orbicularis oris (facial muscle)','Closes and purses the lips']])]],
['The oral cavity',[
'The mouth begins at the lips and includes the **vestibule** (the space between the lips or cheeks and the teeth) and the **oral cavity proper**. Landmarks include the **labial frenum** (a fold attaching the lip to the gingiva), the **mucobuccal fold** where cheek mucosa meets the alveolar mucosa, and the **gingiva**, which is divided into the **free (marginal) gingiva**, **attached gingiva** and the **interdental papillae** between teeth. The roof is the bony **hard palate**, with the **incisive papilla** behind the central incisors and ridges called **palatine rugae**, and the muscular **soft palate** ending in the **uvula**. The **tongue** carries filiform, fungiform, foliate and circumvallate **papillae**, many with taste buds, and is attached by the **lingual frenum**. The **palatine tonsils** sit in the throat between the arches.',
label('oral-cavity','Landmarks of the oral cavity',[['upperlip','Upper lip','Forms the anterior boundary of the vestibule.'],['lowerlip','Lower lip','Contains the orbicularis oris.'],['gingiva','Gingiva','Gum tissue around the teeth; healthy gingiva is pink and firm.'],['frenum','Frenum','Fold of tissue attaching the lip or tongue.'],['hardpalate','Hard palate','Bony roof of the mouth with palatine rugae.'],['softpalate','Soft palate','Muscular posterior roof that closes the nasopharynx when swallowing.'],['uvula','Uvula','Hanging tissue at the end of the soft palate.'],['tonsil','Palatine tonsil','Lymphatic tissue at the side of the throat.'],['tongue','Tongue','Muscle with papillae and taste buds.'],['pharynx','Pharynx','Throat behind the oral cavity.']])]],
['Salivary glands, nerves and blood supply',[
'Three pairs of major **salivary glands** produce saliva, which begins starch digestion, lubricates, buffers acids and helps remineralize enamel. The **parotid gland**, the largest, lies in front of the ear; its **Stensen’s (parotid) duct** opens on the cheek opposite the **maxillary second molar**. The **submandibular gland** drains through **Wharton’s duct** at the **sublingual caruncle** under the tongue—a common site of salivary stones. The **sublingual gland**, the smallest, drains through several small ducts in the floor of the mouth. Reduced saliva (**xerostomia**) from medications, radiation or disease raises caries risk.',
'The **trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V)** provides sensation to the face and teeth. Its **maxillary division (V2)** supplies upper teeth through the posterior, middle and anterior **superior alveolar nerves**, and the palate through the **nasopalatine** and **greater palatine** nerves. Its **mandibular division (V3)** supplies the lower teeth through the **inferior alveolar nerve**, which enters the mandibular foramen and gives off the **mental nerve** (lower lip and chin); the **lingual nerve** supplies the tongue, and the **buccal nerve** the cheek and buccal gingiva of lower molars. V3 also controls the muscles of mastication. The **facial nerve (VII)** controls facial expression. Blood reaches the teeth through branches of the **maxillary artery**, a branch of the external carotid artery.',
sort('Maxillary (V2) or mandibular (V3) branch?',['Maxillary division (V2)','Mandibular division (V3)'],[['Posterior superior alveolar nerve',0],['Nasopalatine nerve',0],['Greater palatine nerve',0],['Inferior alveolar nerve',1],['Lingual nerve',1],['Mental nerve',1]])]],
['Applying anatomy in the dental chair',[
'Anatomy explains everyday dental practice. An **inferior alveolar nerve block** places anesthetic near the mandibular foramen and numbs all mandibular teeth on that side, the lower lip and, with the lingual nerve, the tongue; maxillary teeth are usually numbed by **infiltration** because the maxillary bone is thinner and more porous. The parotid duct opening is a landmark during oral examinations, and a blocked Wharton’s duct may swell during meals. Knowing the location of the maxillary sinus explains why an extracted upper molar root can communicate with the sinus. The masseter and temporalis can be palpated during an extraoral examination for tenderness in patients with TMD.',
kase('Interpreting a complaint',{a:{text:'A patient reports a numb lower lip and chin on the left side after a mandibular injection two hours ago. Which nerve is responsible?',choices:[['The mental nerve, a branch of the inferior alveolar nerve','b','Correct. It supplies the lower lip and chin.',true],['The posterior superior alveolar nerve','b','That nerve supplies maxillary molars.',false]]},b:{text:'The patient also reports swelling under the tongue whenever they eat. Which duct may be blocked?',choices:[['Wharton’s (submandibular) duct','c','Correct. Salivary stones commonly form there.',true],['Stensen’s duct','c','Stensen’s duct opens on the cheek opposite the maxillary second molar.',false]]},c:{text:'The assistant records both findings for the dentist, who examines the floor of the mouth and plans imaging.',end:true}})]],
["The extraoral and intraoral examination",["Anatomy becomes a clinical skill during the **oral examination**, which also serves as an oral cancer screening. The **extraoral** portion begins as the patient walks in: facial symmetry, skin color and lesions, and the lips. The examiner then **palpates** the lymph nodes of the head and neck (submental, submandibular, cervical), the **TMJ** while the patient opens and closes (listening and feeling for clicking, popping or deviation) and the **muscles of mastication**, especially the masseter and temporalis, for tenderness. The **intraoral** portion inspects and palpates the lips, labial and buccal mucosa, the **parotid duct** openings, the vestibules and frena, the hard and soft palate, the oropharynx and tonsils, the **tongue** (dorsal, lateral borders and ventral surface—gauze is used to gently pull the tongue to view its sides) and the **floor of the mouth**, where Wharton’s ducts open. Findings are described by location, size, color, texture and whether the lesion is raised, flat or ulcerated.","The **dentition** and **periodontium** are then examined and charted. Dental assistants prepare the patient, set up the basic tray and record findings exactly as the dentist dictates using correct anatomic terms—for example, “a 3-millimeter white lesion on the left lateral border of the tongue.”",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"A systematic oral examination","steps":["Observe the face, skin and lips","Palpate lymph nodes of the head and neck","Evaluate the TMJ and muscles of mastication","Inspect lips, labial and buccal mucosa and vestibules","Inspect the hard and soft palate, oropharynx and tonsils","Examine all surfaces of the tongue and the floor of the mouth","Examine and chart the teeth and periodontium"]}]]
],takeaways:['Mandible parts: body, ramus, angle, coronoid process, condyle, mental and mandibular foramina, symphysis; the TMJ hinges and glides.','Masseter, temporalis and medial pterygoid close; lateral pterygoid opens and protrudes.','Parotid–Stensen’s duct opposite maxillary second molar; submandibular–Wharton’s duct; V2 supplies upper teeth, V3 (inferior alveolar) lower teeth.'],terms:[['Symphysis','Midline where the two halves of the mandible fuse.'],['Xerostomia','Dry mouth from reduced saliva.'],['Inferior alveolar nerve','V3 branch supplying mandibular teeth.']],mcqs:[q('What is the area where two bones fused at the midline of the mandible?','Symphysis','Condyle','Ramus','Coronoid notch','A Robinson sample question.'),q('Which muscle opens the jaw and protrudes the mandible?','Lateral pterygoid','Masseter','Temporalis','Medial pterygoid','The others mainly elevate.'),q('Stensen’s duct opens opposite the:','Maxillary second molar','Mandibular central incisor','Hard palate','Uvula','It drains the parotid gland.'),q('Which nerve supplies the mandibular teeth?','Inferior alveolar nerve','Nasopalatine nerve','Greater palatine nerve','Facial nerve','It enters the mandibular foramen.'),q('The TMJ is formed by the condyle and the:','Glenoid fossa of the temporal bone','Zygomatic arch','Maxillary sinus','Hyoid bone','An articular disc lies between them.'),q('The largest salivary gland is the:','Parotid','Sublingual','Submandibular','Lacrimal','It lies in front of the ear.'),q('Which nerve division supplies the maxillary teeth?','Maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve (V2)','Mandibular division (V3)','Facial nerve (VII)','Vagus nerve (X)','Superior alveolar nerves are V2 branches.'),q('The ridges on the anterior hard palate are called:','Palatine rugae','Papillae','Frena','Tonsils','They help grip food.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how the anatomy of the mandible, its nerves and the TMJ relates to chewing and to dental anesthesia.',rubric:[['Mandible landmarks',['ramus','body','condyle','coronoid','mandibular foramen','mental foramen']],['TMJ and muscles',['tmj','hinge','glid','masseter','temporalis','pterygoid']],['Nerve supply',['trigeminal','v3','inferior alveolar','mental','lingual']],['Anesthesia application',['block','infiltration','numb','lip','tongue']]],model:'The mandible has a horizontal body holding the lower teeth and a vertical ramus with a coronoid process, where the temporalis attaches, and a condyle that forms the TMJ with the glenoid fossa of the temporal bone. The joint hinges as the mouth opens and then glides forward onto the articular eminence, moved by the masseter, temporalis and medial pterygoid, which close the jaw, and the lateral pterygoid, which opens and protrudes it. Sensation to the lower teeth comes from the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve: the inferior alveolar nerve enters the mandibular foramen on the inner ramus and travels through the bone, giving off the mental nerve at the mental foramen to the lower lip and chin, while the lingual nerve supplies the tongue. An inferior alveolar nerve block deposits anesthetic near the mandibular foramen to numb all teeth on that side, the lip and, with the lingual nerve, the tongue; maxillary teeth are usually anesthetized by infiltration because maxillary bone is thinner.'},sources:[RULES,OS_SKULL,OS_MOUTH]};

export const D3={title:'Tooth morphology and charting',hook:'Tooth number 3 in the Universal system is tooth 16 in FDI notation—the same maxillary right first molar. Dental teams chart thousands of teeth, surfaces and restorations, and a single numbering mistake can lead to treating the wrong tooth. Morphology and notation are the shared language of dentistry.',objectives:['Describe the tissues of the tooth and its supporting structures.','Compare primary and permanent dentitions and eruption timing.','Use the Universal, Palmer and FDI numbering systems.','Name tooth surfaces and distinguishing features of each tooth type.'],sections:[
['Tooth tissues and supporting structures',[
'Each tooth has a **crown** covered by **enamel**, the hardest substance in the body (about 96% mineral), and a **root** covered by **cementum**. **Dentin**, a yellowish living tissue with microscopic tubules, forms the bulk of the tooth beneath enamel and cementum and transmits sensation. The **pulp** in the center contains nerves and blood vessels and enters through the **apical foramen** at the **apex**. Enamel meets cementum at the **cementoenamel junction (CEJ)**. The **periodontium** supports the tooth: **gingiva**, the **periodontal ligament (PDL)** that suspends the tooth in its socket and absorbs force, **cementum** and **alveolar bone**.',
label('tooth','Explore the tooth',[['enamel','Enamel','Hardest tissue; covers the crown.'],['dentin','Dentin','Bulk of the tooth; contains tubules.'],['pulp','Pulp','Nerves and blood vessels.'],['cementum','Cementum','Covers the root; anchors PDL fibers.'],['pdl','Periodontal ligament','Suspends the tooth and absorbs force.'],['bone','Alveolar bone','Socket bone surrounding the root.'],['gingiva','Gingiva','Gum tissue around the neck of the tooth.'],['cej','Cementoenamel junction','Where enamel meets cementum.'],['crown','Crown','Part covered by enamel.'],['root','Root','Part embedded in bone.'],['apex','Apex','Root tip where vessels enter the pulp.']])]],
['Dentitions and eruption',[
'Humans have two dentitions. The **primary (deciduous) dentition** has **20 teeth**—incisors, canines and molars, but **no premolars**—beginning with the mandibular central incisors at about 6–10 months and complete by about age 2–3. The **permanent dentition** has **32 teeth**: in each quadrant, two incisors, one canine, two premolars and three molars. The **mixed dentition** period, from about age 6 to 12, includes both. The **permanent first molar** erupts at about **6 years** behind the primary teeth (the “six-year molar”) and is often mistaken for a primary tooth; second molars erupt at about 12, and **third molars** (wisdom teeth) between about 17 and 21, if at all. Permanent premolars **succeed** (replace) the primary molars.',
{t:'w',kind:'toothchart',title:'Interactive dental chart',prompt:'Click teeth to see the Universal, Palmer and FDI numbers, then switch to Quiz me. Compare the primary and permanent dentitions.'}]],
['Numbering systems',[
'The **Universal system**, used most in the United States, numbers permanent teeth **1–32** starting with the **maxillary right third molar** (tooth 1), moving across the maxillary arch to the maxillary left third molar (16), dropping to the mandibular left third molar (17) and ending at the mandibular right third molar (32). Primary teeth are lettered **A–T** in the same direction. The **Palmer notation** uses a bracket showing the quadrant with numbers 1–8 from the midline (letters A–E for primary teeth). The **FDI (International) two-digit system** gives the **quadrant first**—1 maxillary right, 2 maxillary left, 3 mandibular left, 4 mandibular right (5–8 for primary)—then the tooth, 1–8 from the midline. So the maxillary right central incisor is Universal 8, FDI 11; the mandibular left first molar is Universal 19, FDI 36.',
match('Match the Universal number to the tooth',[['1','Maxillary right third molar'],['8','Maxillary right central incisor'],['11','Maxillary left canine'],['16','Maxillary left third molar'],['19','Mandibular left first molar'],['30','Mandibular right first molar']]),
chk('What is the FDI number for the maxillary left first molar (Universal 14)?',['26','14','36','16'],'Quadrant 2 (maxillary left), tooth 6 (first molar).')]],
['Surfaces and landmarks',[
'Each tooth has five surfaces. The surface toward the lips or cheek is **facial**—**labial** on anterior teeth and **buccal** on posterior teeth. The surface toward the tongue is **lingual** (on maxillary teeth also called **palatal**). The surface toward the midline is **mesial**, and away from it **distal**; adjacent teeth touch at **contact areas** on their proximal (mesial and distal) surfaces. The chewing surface of posterior teeth is **occlusal**, and the biting edge of anterior teeth is **incisal**. Posterior occlusal surfaces have **cusps**, **ridges** (marginal, triangular and the **oblique ridge** of maxillary molars), **fossae**, **grooves** and **pits**—where caries often begins.',
label('surfaces','Name the tooth surfaces',[['labial','Labial','Facial surface of anterior teeth.'],['buccal','Buccal','Facial surface of posterior teeth.'],['lingual','Lingual','Surface toward the tongue.'],['mesial','Mesial','Proximal surface toward the midline.'],['distal','Distal','Proximal surface away from the midline.'],['occlusal','Occlusal','Chewing surface of posterior teeth.'],['incisal','Incisal','Biting edge of anterior teeth.'],['cervical','Cervical','Neck of the tooth near the CEJ.'],['midline','Midline','Imaginary line dividing right and left.']]),
label('molar-occlusal','Occlusal anatomy of a maxillary molar',[['mb','Mesiobuccal cusp','Its tip occludes in the buccal groove of the mandibular first molar in Class I occlusion.'],['db','Distobuccal cusp','Second buccal cusp.'],['ml','Mesiolingual cusp','Largest cusp; the cusp of Carabelli may appear on it.'],['dl','Distolingual cusp','Smallest major cusp.'],['oblique','Oblique ridge','Unique to maxillary molars.'],['mmr','Mesial marginal ridge','Border of the occlusal table.'],['dmr','Distal marginal ridge','Border of the occlusal table.'],['cfossa','Central fossa','Depression where grooves meet.'],['bgroove','Buccal groove','Separates the buccal cusps.']])]],
['Tooth types and distinguishing features',[
'**Incisors** cut food; maxillary central incisors are the widest anterior teeth, and newly erupted incisors show **mamelons** on the incisal edge. **Canines** tear food, have the **longest roots** in the mouth and are called the cornerstones of the arch. **Premolars** crush and grind; the **maxillary first premolar** usually has **two roots** (buccal and lingual), and the mandibular second premolar may have three cusps. **Maxillary molars** usually have **three roots**—mesiobuccal, distobuccal and palatal—and an **oblique ridge**; the maxillary first molar may have a **cusp of Carabelli**. **Mandibular molars** usually have **two roots** (mesial and distal); the mandibular first molar has **five cusps**. Primary teeth have thinner enamel, larger pulps, whiter crowns and widely flared roots that make room for developing permanent teeth.',
sort('Maxillary molar or mandibular molar?',['Maxillary molar','Mandibular molar'],[['Three roots including a palatal root',0],['Oblique ridge',0],['Possible cusp of Carabelli',0],['Two roots, mesial and distal',1],['First molar usually has five cusps',1],['Tooth numbers 18–19 and 30–31',1]])]],
["Charting conditions and restorations",["**Dental charting** records existing conditions and treatment needed using symbols on an anatomic or geometric chart. Many offices use **red** for conditions that need treatment (such as caries) and **blue or black** for completed treatment (existing restorations), although electronic charts use their own symbols. Common notations include an **X** through a missing tooth, a circled area for caries, outlined and filled surfaces for amalgam or composite restorations, lines for root canal treatment, and symbols for crowns, bridges, implants, sealants and impacted or drifting teeth. Surfaces are abbreviated with their initials: **M** (mesial), **O** (occlusal), **D** (distal), **B** (buccal), **L** (lingual), **F** (facial), **I** (incisal). Multi-surface restorations combine them in order from mesial to distal, such as **MO**, **DO** and **MOD**, and anterior restorations may be noted as **MI** or **DL**.","Periodontal charting records **probing depths** at six sites per tooth, **recession**, **bleeding on probing**, **mobility** (graded 0–3) and **furcation involvement** in multirooted teeth. Occlusion is recorded with Angle’s classification. Accuracy is essential: the assistant repeats unclear dictation, confirms the tooth number and surfaces and never guesses. A chart error can lead to treatment on the wrong tooth, so the final chart is reviewed by the dentist.",{"t":"check","q":"A restoration involves the mesial, occlusal and distal surfaces of tooth 14. How is it charted?","options":["14 MOD","14 DOM","14 OMD","14 MD"],"why":"Combine surfaces from mesial to distal."},{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Surface abbreviation","buckets":["Posterior tooth surface","Anterior tooth surface"],"items":[["O (occlusal)",0],["B (buccal)",0],["I (incisal)",1],["F or labial",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Enamel covers the crown, cementum the root, dentin forms the bulk and the pulp holds nerves and vessels; the PDL suspends the tooth.','20 primary teeth (A–T, no premolars) and 32 permanent (1–32 starting at the maxillary right third molar); FDI gives quadrant then tooth.','Surfaces: facial (labial/buccal), lingual, mesial, distal, occlusal/incisal; maxillary molars have three roots, mandibular molars two.'],terms:[['Cementoenamel junction','Line where enamel meets cementum.'],['Mixed dentition','Period with primary and permanent teeth present.'],['Succedaneous tooth','Permanent tooth that replaces a primary tooth.']],mcqs:[q('Which is the hardest tissue in the body?','Enamel','Dentin','Cementum','Bone','It is about 96% mineral.'),q('How many teeth are in the primary dentition?','20','32','24','28','There are no primary premolars.'),q('In the Universal system, tooth 1 is the:','Maxillary right third molar','Mandibular left third molar','Maxillary right central incisor','Maxillary left third molar','Numbering starts at the upper right.'),q('In FDI notation, tooth 36 is the:','Mandibular left first molar','Maxillary left first molar','Mandibular right canine','Maxillary right third molar','Quadrant 3 is mandibular left.'),q('The surface of a posterior tooth facing the cheek is:','Buccal','Labial','Lingual','Incisal','Labial is used for anterior teeth.'),q('Which tooth has the longest root?','Canine','Central incisor','First premolar','Third molar','Canines are cornerstones of the arch.'),q('Maxillary molars typically have how many roots?','Three','One','Two','Four','Mesiobuccal, distobuccal and palatal.'),q('The permanent first molar erupts at about age:','6','12','18','2','It is called the six-year molar.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient’s chart notes a restoration on tooth 30 MO. Explain what this means, give the tooth’s FDI number, and describe the anatomy you would expect for this tooth.',rubric:[['Identify the tooth',['mandibular right first molar','30']],['Surfaces',['mesial','occlusal','mo']],['FDI number',['46']],['Expected anatomy',['two roots','mesial','distal','five cusps','enamel','pulp']]],model:'In the Universal system, tooth 30 is the mandibular right first molar. “MO” means the restoration involves the mesial (toward the midline) and occlusal (chewing) surfaces, so it is a two-surface restoration that includes a proximal surface. In FDI notation, the tooth is 46: quadrant 4 is the mandibular right, and 6 is the first molar. As a mandibular first molar, it usually has two roots, mesial and distal, and five cusps on its occlusal surface, with enamel covering the crown, dentin beneath, a pulp chamber with canals in each root, and cementum covering the roots, which are suspended in alveolar bone by the periodontal ligament. It erupts at about age six and is a key tooth for occlusion.'},sources:[RULES,OS_MOUTH,ADEA]};

export const D4={title:'Dental pathology, prevention and oral hygiene instruction',hook:'Tooth decay is one of the most common chronic diseases in the world, yet it is largely preventable. Dental professionals recognize disease early—caries, gum disease, oral lesions and cancer—and teach patients the brushing and flossing techniques that prevent it, which is HOSA Skill V.',objectives:['Explain the caries process and classify lesions with G. V. Black’s system.','Distinguish gingivitis from periodontitis.','Recognize common oral lesions, developmental anomalies and malocclusions.','Teach brushing and flossing following HOSA Skill V.'],sections:[
['Dental caries',[
'**Caries** is the bacterial disease that causes cavities. Bacteria in **plaque** (biofilm), especially *Streptococcus mutans* and lactobacilli, ferment sugars and produce acid that lowers the pH at the tooth surface. Below a critical pH of about 5.5, minerals dissolve from enamel (**demineralization**); saliva, fluoride and time allow **remineralization**. An early **incipient lesion** appears as a chalky **white spot** and can be reversed; once a cavity forms, the tooth needs restoration. Risk factors include frequent sugar or acid intake, poor oral hygiene, **xerostomia**, deep pits and fissures and lack of fluoride. **Early childhood caries** often affects maxillary incisors of young children who sleep with bottles of milk or juice; **root caries** affects older adults with gum recession.',
'G. V. Black classified carious lesions by location: **Class I**—pits and fissures (occlusal surfaces of posterior teeth, buccal or lingual pits, lingual pits of maxillary incisors); **Class II**—proximal surfaces of posterior teeth; **Class III**—proximal surfaces of anterior teeth not involving the incisal angle; **Class IV**—proximal surfaces of anterior teeth including the incisal angle; **Class V**—the cervical third of facial or lingual surfaces; **Class VI**—incisal edges of anterior teeth or cusp tips of posterior teeth.',
label('cavity-classes','G. V. Black cavity classifications',[['c1','Class I','Pits and fissures.'],['c2','Class II','Proximal surfaces of posterior teeth.'],['c3','Class III','Proximal anterior, not the incisal angle.'],['c4','Class IV','Proximal anterior including the incisal angle.'],['c5','Class V','Cervical third of facial or lingual surfaces.'],['c6','Class VI','Incisal edges or cusp tips.']])]],
['Periodontal disease',[
'**Gingivitis** is inflammation of the gingiva caused by plaque: red, swollen gums that **bleed easily**. It is **reversible** with good oral hygiene and professional cleaning. Untreated, it can progress to **periodontitis**, in which inflammation destroys the **periodontal ligament** and **alveolar bone**, creating **periodontal pockets** deeper than the normal 1–3 mm sulcus, **gum recession**, **tooth mobility** and eventually tooth loss. **Calculus** (tartar) is mineralized plaque that must be removed by scaling. Risk factors include smoking, diabetes, genetics, stress and certain medications, and periodontitis is associated with cardiovascular disease and poor diabetes control. Periodontal charting records probing depths at six sites per tooth using a **periodontal probe** marked in millimeters.',
label('perio','Healthy and diseased periodontium',[['gingiva','Gingiva','Healthy tissue is pink, firm and stippled.'],['sulcus','Sulcus','Normal space between tooth and gingiva, 1–3 mm.'],['pocket','Periodontal pocket','Deepened sulcus over 3 mm from tissue destruction.'],['calculus','Calculus','Mineralized plaque that harbors bacteria.'],['probe','Periodontal probe','Measures depth in millimeters.'],['boneloss','Bone loss','Destruction of alveolar bone in periodontitis.'],['bone','Alveolar bone','Supports the tooth.'],['toothroot','Root surface','Covered by cementum.']]),
sort('Gingivitis or periodontitis?',['Gingivitis','Periodontitis'],[['Red gums that bleed on brushing',0],['Reversible with good oral hygiene',0],['No bone loss',0],['Pockets of 5–6 mm',1],['Bone loss on radiographs',1],['Loose teeth',1]])]],
['Oral lesions, cancer and anomalies',[
'Dental teams perform an **oral cancer screening** at examinations. **Leukoplakia** is a white patch that **cannot be wiped off** and may be precancerous; **erythroplakia** (red patch) carries even higher risk. **Candidiasis (thrush)** is a fungal infection with white patches that **can be wiped off**, common in infants, denture wearers and immunocompromised patients. **Herpes labialis** (cold sores) is caused by herpes simplex virus and is contagious; **aphthous ulcers** (canker sores) occur inside the mouth and are not contagious. **Oral cancer**, usually squamous cell carcinoma, often appears on the lateral tongue or floor of the mouth; risk factors include tobacco, alcohol and **HPV**. A sore that does not heal within two weeks should be evaluated. A **periapical abscess** is a pus-filled infection at the root tip from pulp death.',
'Developmental anomalies include **anodontia** (missing teeth), **supernumerary** teeth such as a **mesiodens** between the maxillary central incisors, **microdontia** such as a peg lateral incisor, **fusion** and **gemination**, **amelogenesis imperfecta** and **dentinogenesis imperfecta**, **fluorosis** (mottled enamel from excess fluoride during development), **cleft lip and palate** and **ankyloglossia** (tongue-tie). **Malocclusion** is classified by Edward Angle: **Class I (neutroclusion)**—the mesiobuccal cusp of the maxillary first molar occludes in the buccal groove of the mandibular first molar; **Class II (distoclusion)**—the mandible is retruded; **Class III (mesioclusion)**—the mandible is protruded. Wear includes **attrition** (tooth-to-tooth, often from bruxism), **abrasion** (from hard brushing or objects) and **erosion** (from acids).',
match('Match the finding to the condition',[['White patch that wipes off','Candidiasis'],['White patch that cannot be wiped off','Leukoplakia'],['Contagious blister on the lip border','Herpes labialis'],['Painful ulcer inside the cheek, not contagious','Aphthous ulcer'],['Extra tooth between maxillary central incisors','Mesiodens'],['Mandible protruded relative to maxilla','Class III malocclusion']])]],
['Prevention',[
'**Fluoride** strengthens enamel, promotes remineralization and reduces acid production; sources include community water fluoridation, fluoride toothpaste, varnish and professional applications. **Sealants**—resin coatings on pits and fissures of molars—prevent Class I caries. Diet counseling focuses on **how often** sugar and acids are consumed, not just how much. Patients brush twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, clean between teeth daily and visit the dentist regularly. Mouthguards protect against sports injuries, and tobacco cessation reduces gum disease and oral cancer.']],
['Oral hygiene instruction (HOSA Skill V)',[
'In Skill V, the competitor assembles materials, performs hand hygiene, puts on PPE, greets and identifies the patient and **explains the importance of correctly brushing**, suggesting a **soft-textured brush** unless the dentist recommends otherwise. Using a toothbrush and model, they demonstrate: begin at the **facial surface of the right rear teeth** and brush each tooth systematically; place the brush at a **45° angle to the gumline**; rotate the brush slightly and push bristles gently between the teeth; use **very short, back-and-forth, light vibrating movements**; place the brush **vertically** for lingual surfaces of anterior teeth; and clean biting surfaces with short vibrating motions. They ask if the patient understands or has questions.',
'For flossing, they explain its importance, remove **12–18 inches of floss**, wrap it around the **middle fingers** of both hands to anchor it, unroll new floss from one middle finger as it is used, use **index finger and thumb (or two thumbs)** for **maxillary** teeth and **both index fingers** for **mandibular** teeth, keep fingers about **1–2 inches apart**, gently insert floss between the teeth **without snapping** into the gums, slide it into the space between tooth and gum until resistance is felt, curve it into a **C shape** around the tooth, hold it tightly and move it away from the gum up and down, repeat for all teeth, use clean floss if it becomes soiled and explain that **some bleeding and soreness may occur** the first few times—but if it continues, stop and notify the doctor. The skill ends with checking understanding, communication, cleaning the typodont, removing PPE and hand hygiene (46 points; 70% is 32.2).',
order('Flossing steps (HOSA Skill V)',['Remove 12–18 inches of floss','Wrap floss around the middle fingers','Use thumb and index finger for maxillary teeth; index fingers for mandibular','Keep 1–2 inches of floss between fingers','Insert gently between teeth without snapping','Curve into a C shape around the tooth','Move up and down away from the gum','Use clean floss as you move to each tooth'])]]
],takeaways:['Caries: plaque bacteria make acid below pH ~5.5; white spots can remineralize; Black’s Classes I–VI by location.','Gingivitis is reversible bleeding inflammation; periodontitis destroys bone with pockets over 3 mm.','Skill V: soft brush at 45°, short vibrating strokes, vertical for anterior lingual; floss 12–18 in, C shape, warn about early bleeding.'],terms:[['Demineralization','Loss of minerals from enamel caused by acid.'],['Leukoplakia','White patch that cannot be wiped off; may be precancerous.'],['Neutroclusion','Angle Class I relationship of the first molars.']],mcqs:[q('A Class II lesion involves:','Proximal surfaces of posterior teeth','Pits and fissures','Cervical third of facial surfaces','Incisal edges','Class II restorations often need a matrix band.'),q('Which condition is reversible?','Gingivitis','Periodontitis with bone loss','Oral cancer','Amelogenesis imperfecta','Good hygiene reverses gingivitis.'),q('A white patch that wipes off is most likely:','Candidiasis','Leukoplakia','Erythroplakia','Lichen planus','Leukoplakia does not wipe off.'),q('In Skill V, the brush is placed at what angle to the gumline?','45°','90°','10°','180°','Short vibrating strokes follow.'),q('How much floss is removed in Skill V?','12–18 inches','2–4 inches','36 inches','6 feet','It is wrapped around the middle fingers.'),q('Which bacterium is most associated with caries?','Streptococcus mutans','Escherichia coli','Candida albicans','Staphylococcus epidermidis','It ferments sugars to acid.'),q('Class III malocclusion means:','The mandible is protruded (mesioclusion)','Normal molar relationship','The mandible is retruded','Missing teeth','Class II is distoclusion.'),q('What should the patient be told about bleeding when flossing?','Some bleeding may occur at first; if it continues, stop and notify the doctor','Bleeding always means flossing is wrong','Bleeding never happens','Floss harder to stop bleeding','This is a Skill V step.')],frq:{prompt:'A 15-year-old has bleeding gums and two white spot lesions near the gumline. Explain the disease processes involved, whether they are reversible, and the oral hygiene instruction you would give following HOSA Skill V.',rubric:[['Gingivitis explanation',['gingivitis','plaque','inflam','bleed','reversible']],['Caries and white spots',['demineraliz','acid','white spot','fluoride','reminer']],['Brushing instruction',['soft','45','short','vibrat','vertical','systematic']],['Flossing instruction and follow-up',['12','18','c shape','snap','bleeding','question']]],model:'The bleeding gums indicate gingivitis, inflammation caused by plaque bacteria at the gumline, which is reversible with good oral hygiene and professional cleaning before it becomes periodontitis with bone loss. The white spots are incipient caries: acids produced by plaque bacteria fermenting sugars have demineralized the enamel, but because no cavity has formed, fluoride, reduced sugar frequency and better plaque removal can remineralize them. Following Skill V, I would explain why brushing matters, recommend a soft brush and demonstrate on a model starting at the facial surface of the right rear teeth, holding the brush at 45° to the gumline, pushing bristles gently between teeth with short, light vibrating strokes, using a vertical brush for anterior lingual surfaces and cleaning biting surfaces. For flossing, I would remove 12–18 inches, wrap it on the middle fingers, use thumbs and index fingers for upper teeth and index fingers for lower teeth with 1–2 inches between, insert gently without snapping, wrap in a C shape and move up and down, use clean sections, and warn that some bleeding may occur at first but to stop and notify the dentist if it continues, then ask if they have questions.'},sources:[RULES,NIDCR_DECAY,NIDCR_GUM,NIDCR_CANCER,MH_BRUSH]};

export const D5={title:'Infection control and occupational safety',hook:'Dental procedures create spatter and aerosols, use sharp instruments and involve saliva and blood in every appointment. Infection control is how the dental office protects patients and staff, and three Round Two skills—surface barriers, the ultrasonic cleaner and PPE—test it directly.',objectives:['Apply standard precautions and OSHA requirements in the dental office.','Don and doff PPE following HOSA Skill VIII.','Place and remove surface barriers following HOSA Skill IV.','Process instruments, including operating the ultrasonic cleaner (Skill VII), and monitor sterilization.'],sections:[
['Standard precautions and OSHA',[
'**Standard precautions** treat blood, saliva and other body fluids of **every patient** as potentially infectious. They include hand hygiene, PPE, respiratory hygiene, sharps safety, safe injection practices, sterilization of instruments and cleaning and disinfection of surfaces. The **OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard** requires employers to have a written **exposure control plan**, provide PPE at no cost, offer the **hepatitis B vaccine** free to employees with exposure risk, use engineering controls such as sharps containers and safety devices, train employees annually and provide post-exposure evaluation. The **OSHA Hazard Communication Standard** requires labeled chemicals, **Safety Data Sheets (SDS)** and training on chemical hazards. The **CDC** publishes infection prevention guidance for dental settings, including keeping **dental unit waterlines** at or below **500 CFU/mL** of heterotrophic bacteria.',
sort('OSHA requirement or CDC recommendation?',['OSHA (worker safety law)','CDC (infection prevention guidance)'],[['Employer offers hepatitis B vaccine free to at-risk staff',0],['Written exposure control plan',0],['Safety Data Sheets for chemicals',0],['Dental waterline water at or below 500 CFU/mL',1],['Weekly biological monitoring of sterilizers',1],['Employer provides PPE at no cost',0]])]],
['Personal protective equipment (HOSA Skill VIII)',[
'In Skill VIII, the competitor performs hand hygiene and dons the **gown** so it fully covers the torso from neck to knees and arms to the end of the wrists, wrapping around the back, and fastens it at the neck and waist; the **mask**, securing ties or elastic at the middle of the head and neck or around the ears, fitting the flexible band to the nose bridge and fitting it snugly to the face and below the chin; and **eyewear** (facewear) over the face and eyes, adjusted over the mask. After **hand hygiene**, gloves are put on so they **extend over the gown cuffs**.',
'For removal, the competitor takes off **gloves** first—grasping the palm of one gloved hand, peeling it off, holding it in the gloved hand, sliding ungloved fingers under the remaining glove at the wrist and pulling down to turn it inside out—and discards them. **Eyewear** is removed from the back touching only the ear rests and placed on a disposable towel until cleaned and disinfected. The **mask** is removed by sliding fingers under the elastic in front of the ears (or untying) and discarded. The **gown** is unfastened without letting the sleeves touch the body, pulled away from the neck and shoulders touching only the inside, turned inside out, folded into a bundle and discarded. The skill ends with hand hygiene (50 points; 70% is 35). Note that this dental rating sheet removes the mask **before** the gown; always follow the sequence of the sheet you are judged on.',
order('Doffing order in Dental Skill VIII',['Remove gloves, turning them inside out','Remove eyewear by the ear rests','Remove the mask by the elastic or ties','Remove the gown, touching only the inside','Perform hand hygiene'])]],
['Surface barriers (HOSA Skill IV)',[
'**Clinical contact surfaces**—light handles, chair controls, air-water syringe, bracket table—are either covered with **barriers** or **cleaned and disinfected** between patients. Barriers save time and protect surfaces that are hard to clean, but they must be changed for every patient. In Skill IV, the competitor enters a cleaned and disinfected room, performs hand hygiene, **selects appropriate barriers**, places each barrier **over the entire surface** to be protected and **checks that each is secure**. When the judge says the procedure is complete, the competitor performs hand hygiene, dons PPE and **utility gloves**, and **carefully removes each cover without touching the underlying surface** or the contaminated outer surface; if a surface is touched inadvertently, the competitor recognizes it and verbalizes the need to disinfect it. Barriers go in the **regular trash** unless the scenario indicates regulated waste under state law. Utility gloves are washed, disinfected and dried, PPE removed and hand hygiene performed (22 points; 70% is 15.4).',
kase('A touched surface',{a:{text:'While removing the barrier from the light handle, your utility glove brushes the bare handle. What should you do?',choices:[['Recognize it and verbalize that the handle must now be cleaned and disinfected','b','Correct. The rating sheet awards the step when you recognize the contamination.',true],['Ignore it because the barrier protected most of the handle','b','The touched area is now contaminated.',false]]},b:{text:'Where do the used barriers go?',choices:[['Regular trash, unless the scenario indicates regulated waste under state law','c','Correct. Barriers are usually not regulated waste.',true],['The sharps container','c','Sharps containers are for sharps only.',false]]},c:{text:'You disinfect the handle, wash and disinfect your utility gloves, remove PPE and perform hand hygiene.',end:true}})]],
['Instrument processing and sterilization',[
'Instruments follow a one-way **processing cycle** to prevent cross-contamination: **transport** in a covered container, **cleaning** (ultrasonic cleaner or instrument washer—never hand scrubbing when avoidable), **drying**, **packaging** in pouches or wrapped cassettes with chemical indicators, **sterilization**, **storage** in a clean, dry area and **delivery** to the chairside. The **Spaulding classification** guides processing: **critical** items that penetrate tissue or bone (forceps, scalers, burs, surgical instruments) are **sterilized**; **semicritical** items that touch mucous membranes (mouth mirrors, amalgam condensers, handpieces) are **heat-sterilized** whenever possible; **noncritical** items that touch intact skin (blood pressure cuff, X-ray tube head) receive low- or intermediate-level disinfection. Sterilization methods include **steam autoclave**, **unsaturated chemical vapor** and **dry heat**. Sterilization is monitored with **physical** indicators (time, temperature, pressure readings), **chemical** indicators (color change on packaging and inside packs) and **biological indicators (spore tests)**, at least **weekly**.',
'In Skill VII, the competitor puts on gown, mask, eyewear and **utility gloves**, removes the lid, confirms the unit is filled to the manufacturer’s level, places **loose instruments** in the beaker, basket or pan, **replaces the lid** (which limits aerosols) and verifies the solution **covers the articles completely**. They turn the unit on, verbalize turning the timer past 5 minutes and then setting the cleaning time from the chart, verbalize checking for **bubbles** (cavitation) to confirm it is working and that the unit runs **5–15 minutes**. Afterward, they lift articles with **transfer forceps**, place them on paper towels, **rinse thoroughly**, verbalize air-drying or drying with paper towels, clean and disinfect the work area, wash and disinfect utility gloves, remove PPE and perform hand hygiene (26 points; 70% is 18.2).',
match('Match the Spaulding category to its processing',[['Extraction forceps (critical)','Sterilization'],['Mouth mirror (semicritical)','Heat sterilization when possible'],['Blood pressure cuff (noncritical)','Low- or intermediate-level disinfection'],['Spore test','Biological monitoring at least weekly'],['Color-change tape','Chemical indicator'],['Ultrasonic cleaner','Removes debris by cavitation before sterilization']])]],
['Other occupational hazards',[
'**Sharps injuries** are prevented by using safety devices, **never recapping with two hands** (use a one-handed scoop or recapping device), disposing of needles and blades immediately in **puncture-resistant sharps containers** and removing burs from handpieces after use. After an exposure, wash the area with soap and water, report it immediately and receive medical evaluation. **Chemical hazards** include disinfectants, mercury in amalgam and acids; follow the SDS, wear PPE and ventilate. **Mercury hygiene** includes using precapsulated amalgam, a no-touch technique, storing scrap amalgam in sealed containers and using **amalgam separators**. **Radiation** safety is covered in radiology. **Ergonomic** hazards—awkward postures and repetitive motions—are reduced with correct stool height, neutral wrist positions and stretching. Latex allergy is prevented with non-latex gloves and asking about allergies.',
chk('After an instrument stick, what is the first action?',['Wash the area with soap and water, then report immediately','Finish the procedure first','Squeeze the wound and apply bleach','Wait to see if symptoms appear'],'Prompt washing, reporting and evaluation are essential.')]]
],takeaways:['Standard precautions for every patient; OSHA requires an exposure control plan, free hepatitis B vaccine, PPE and SDS.','Skill VIII: don gown, mask, eyewear, hand hygiene, gloves; doff gloves, eyewear, mask, gown, hand hygiene.','Skill IV barriers cover whole surfaces and are removed with utility gloves; Skill VII ultrasonic: lid on, solution covers, 5–15 minutes, transfer forceps, rinse; spore tests weekly.'],terms:[['Cavitation','Bubble formation and collapse that cleans in an ultrasonic unit.'],['Biological indicator','Spore test that confirms sterilization.'],['Clinical contact surface','Surface touched during treatment.']],mcqs:[q('Which item is classified as critical?','Extraction forceps','Blood pressure cuff','Mouth mirror','Patient chair','Critical items penetrate tissue or bone.'),q('How often should sterilizers have biological monitoring?','At least weekly','Once a year','Never','Only when broken','Spore tests confirm sterilization.'),q('In Dental Skill VIII, which item is removed first?','Gloves','Gown','Mask','Eyewear','Gloves are most contaminated.'),q('Surface barriers are removed while wearing:','Utility gloves','No gloves','Sterile gloves','Cotton gloves','Skill IV uses utility gloves.'),q('In Skill VII, how long does the ultrasonic unit typically run?','5–15 minutes','30 seconds','1 hour','24 hours','Follow the cleaning chart.'),q('Why is the lid replaced on the ultrasonic cleaner?','To reduce aerosols and spatter','To heat the solution','To sterilize instruments','It is optional','The lid contains aerosolized solution.'),q('OSHA requires employers to offer which vaccine free to at-risk staff?','Hepatitis B','Influenza','Measles','Tetanus','It is part of the Bloodborne Pathogens Standard.'),q('Dental waterline water should contain no more than:','500 CFU/mL of heterotrophic bacteria','5,000 CFU/mL','50,000 CFU/mL','Any amount','This matches drinking water standards.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how a dental assistant prevents disease transmission between patients from the end of one appointment to the start of the next, including PPE, surface management and instrument processing.',rubric:[['PPE use and removal order',['gloves','eyewear','mask','gown','hand hygiene']],['Surface barriers and disinfection',['barrier','utility gloves','disinfect','underlying surface']],['Instrument processing',['ultrasonic','rinse','package','steriliz','autoclave','storage']],['Monitoring and safety',['spore','biological','chemical indicator','sharps','osha']]],model:'At the end of the appointment, the assistant removes contaminated gloves, eyewear by the ear rests, the mask by its elastic and the gown turned inside out, then performs hand hygiene. Wearing PPE and utility gloves, the assistant carefully removes surface barriers without touching the underlying surfaces, disinfects any surface that was touched or not covered, discards barriers in regular trash unless state law requires otherwise and disposes of sharps in the sharps container. Instruments are transported in a covered container to the sterilization area, placed in the ultrasonic cleaner with the lid on and solution covering them for 5–15 minutes, lifted out with transfer forceps, rinsed, dried, packaged with chemical indicators and sterilized in an autoclave, then stored in a clean, dry area. Sterilization is verified with physical readings, chemical indicators and weekly biological spore tests. Before the next patient, the assistant performs hand hygiene, places fresh barriers over whole surfaces, opens sterile packs and dons new PPE, following OSHA and CDC standards throughout.'},sources:[RULES,OSHA_BBP,CDC_DENTAL]};
